{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789969510,"nonce":"XRrVBhrXn5Oh1LEcm28QNFS4Nt0=","title":"Python: pyflate-fast allocates about 6 GB per iteration and spends a fifth of its time collecting","message":"With the stale-word retention fixed (a0dcdcd), pyflate-fast.py -n 5 completes\non the NUC (x86_64, LLVM tier): iterations 1.05 to 1.57 s against PyPy 0.29\nto 0.34 s and CPython 0.34 to 0.53 s on the same loaded machine, whole\nprocess 12.9 s against PyPy 3.5 s. Peak RSS 288 MB, the pool heap held at\nabout 52 MB.\n\nWhat remains is volume: 905 collections in 5 iterations at the 32 MB floor,\nso about 5.8 GB carved per iteration, 3.06 s of the 13.8 s wall spent in the\ncollector (1 to 12 ms each, about 2 MB reached). ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=3 at a\ntypical collection frees 481k blocks / 31 MB, almost all in the 16 B and\n48 B classes (boxes), with 9 large blocks and a few dozen 32 B list headers\nlive. The kernel's inner loops (bit reads over lists of ints, table\nlookups) are boxing on every step.\n\nTwo directions, both outside the collector: keep the int lists and their\nelement reads unboxed through the kernel's loops (the frontend's shape and\nelement-type inference), and raise the floor for a program whose live set\nis tiny against its allocation rate (the budget is max(2 x live, 32 MB), so\na 2 MB live set collects every 32 MB; a floor proportional to allocation\nrate or a larger fixed floor would cut the count, at the cost of memory).","files":null}]}